{
 "_about": "Concepts the tools map (toolmap.py) uses to match an action against the tools that already do it. Data, not code: extend it in $JOURNAL_HOME/tool_concepts.json (same shape; words and code are added to a concept of the same name, a new name adds a concept). kind: verb (what is done), object (what it is done to or where), method (how a loose script does it). passive: true marks a verb that only reads (research): it shows tools but never stops a call. words: whole words or phrases, any language; plurals and -ing/-ed forms match on their own; file names and paths never count as words (site_publish.sh is a file, not publish). channel: true marks an object that is a place things are published or sent to (a social platform, a store, email, a chat app). needs: channel on a verb means the verb counts only when the action also names a channel (publish alone, as in shipping a website, is not posting); weak: words of the verb that count only when the action itself names the channel (uploading a file to a server is not posting; with a channel only from the session context, a posting word like post is needed). vendors: a tool named after one of these services (supabase-deploy) is shown only when the action names that service. needs may also be a list of concepts, one of which must be named (scrape needs a web or remote source: extracting frames from a local video is not scraping; generate_media needs a paid generation API or a platform: rendering a local demo is not content generation for a channel). needs_own: the channel must come from the action itself, not from the session's last actions (send the site change to the box is not a message). code: regular expressions over commands and file contents, each mapped to one or more concepts.",
 "vendors": ["supabase", "vercel", "netlify", "cloudflare", "firebase", "heroku", "railway", "render.com", "mcpize"],
 "concepts": {
